  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard or waited for the rule to run, so neither has been checked yet. The rule's status is still \"pending\".\n\n**Fields.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) holds web-server request logs. The main fields are:\n- `response` (the HTTP status, stored as text, with a `.keyword` version)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `request` and `url`\n- `agent`\n\n**Dashboard.** \"EFF-ad2bb3fe Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests (a single number)\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Requests by response code (pie chart)\n- Average bytes by file extension (bar chart)\n\nIt's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-ad2bb3fe-web-logs-overview-de78386340f7.\n\n**Alert rule.** \"EFF-ad2bb3fe 5xx spike\" (id `6a1a0418-9b28-45e6-ad2c-3278fff6bbb8`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and counts documents in the last 5 minutes whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`. It fires when that count is above 10.\n\nI didn't attach any notification action, so when it fires it will only show up as an alert in Kibana.",
